A welcome before the first question, and a palace that survives a reboot
=====================================================================

**Setup used to open with "Where should the models run?"** — a question asked by a program that had not
said what it was. Now it opens with that, in one screen: *Welcome to QuixiDesk, driven by VolkDude85's
Nora. We do things a little differently here — starting with this.* Two questions follow, and they are
the two that decide what the app is.

**An "About Nora" button** opens the rest without turning setup into a brochure: what she is (a
companion on your machine, one user, memory you own), what we are aiming for (software that treats the
person in front of it as the authority on their own life, with a persona that is a file and a memory
that is a folder), and — before anything else gets configured — **that this is assistive software**.
The person it was built for is disabled; words go missing mid-sentence and the thread of a
conversation disappears. Nora exists to hold that thread. Under the ADA that is not a disclaimer
attached to the product, it is the point of an effective communication aid.

**And the memory palace no longer defaults to a temporary directory.** It defaulted to
`<app data>/palace`, which under a test profile is `/tmp/...` — memory that a reboot deletes, and, in a
normal install, memory an uninstall takes with it. Both are the opposite of the point. The default is
now `~/memory-palace`: its own named directory in the user's home, outside the application's folder,
surviving both.

Preference order is unchanged and deliberate: the folder chosen at setup, then `QUIXI_PALACE_DIR`, then
this default.

